---
/* Sign-in.
 *
 * This was a 404 while the public header linked to it — the header was wired to
 * a real URL that had never been created, so the primary way into the product
 * from every public page was a dead end for anyone logged out.
 *
 * It is a real URL rather than a fragment for the same reason as the others: it
 * has to be linkable. "Ve a ponrumbo.com/login" is a sentence someone says out
 * loud.
 */
import Public from '../layouts/Public.astro';
import LoginForm from '../components/LoginForm.svelte';
import { SITE_NAME, TUTOR } from '../lib/site.js';
---
<Public
  title={`Entrar — ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description="Entra con tu invitación. El acceso es por invitación mientras estamos en fase alfa."
>
  <div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Tu clase de hoy te espera</div>
    <h1 style="margin-top:10px">Entra y sigue donde lo dejaste.</h1>
    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:10px">
      Entra con tu código de invitación. Cada día: un video corto, algo real que
      hacer, y {TUTOR} leyendo lo que escribiste. Terminas con trabajo tuyo que
      mostrar, no con un certificado.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* client:load, not client:visible: this island IS the page, it is above the
      fold on every device, and a form that appears a beat after the words
      around it is the flicker this migration exists to remove. */}
  {/* Wrapped, and the wrapper is load-bearing. `body.wide .app > *` places
      each direct child in the text column, but Astro gives <astro-island>
      `display:contents` — so the island is not a grid item, its CHILDREN
      become the grid items instead, and they inherit no column. They land in
      the implicit track past `full-end`: the whole form rendered 32px wide
      against the right edge. A real element here takes the placement. */}
  <div>
    <LoginForm client:load />
  </div>
</Public>
